as for the gdp per capita growth, i am importing it as a time series from 1995-2015, and then turning it into a single measure via R. annualised average


the original variable gea7090 was calculated as such in the 1995 working paper: average annual growth in real GDP divided by the economically active population between
the years 1970 and 1990. Exact calculation is 100*(1/20)*ln(GDPEA90/GDPEA70). 


to make the figure, im gonna first calculate the mean for gdp per capita growth rates to get only one column, then append that onto the NRs dataframe. once i do that im going to plot the nr total variable against the annualised average gdp per capita growth variable (with the countries being named of course.) with potentially ggplot2 or baser. and then also the regression. 

interestingly enough, every country that had NRs data in 1995 also had gdp per capita data full for the 1995-2014 period. 

i am using constant 2015 gdp per capita in dollars. 


since we used growth rates data rather than absolute values as in the paper - they had just a start and end point of absolute gdp , the rates are given to us and as such this is a slightly more direct method.
to make sure we take into account the compound effects of growth, 

heres why an arithmetic mean isnt as good a fit as logs: arithmetic mean may give us the "average growth" but this is actually not its direct impact on gdp which means its basically nonsense to frame it as though it does...
a 100% growth followed by -50% means the compound effect is actually 0, which is reflected with logs but not with arithmetic which would return +25%. 

therefore we must adjust the growth formula to calculate its total impact. 

the formula here is quite identical to the original, except that it doesnt take into account the gdp per economically active person but rather population as a simplification and so as to not mix datasets. 
